<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<system>
    <!-- The librefirewall system description for the current QEMU q35
         bring-up: the two-dataplane-port zero-copy virtio-net forwarding slice
         (virtio 1.0 PCI), plus a dedicated management port. THREE instances of
         the same driver binary, one per port; the two dataplane ones are joined
         through a forwarder PD by one pipeline per direction — three memory
         regions each — and the third hands its frames to a management PD that
         forwards nothing. Each dataplane pipeline's buffer pool is DMA'd into by
         the receiving NIC and out of by the transmitting NIC, so a frame crosses
         the whole system without ever being copied. The session-replication and
         mirror ports of the full port model (CONCEPT.md §9) are not part of this
         slice. The protection domains are documented in pds/nic-driver,
         pds/forwarder and pds/management. -->

    <!-- ===================================================================
         The physical address map is a contract, and this is where it is
         stated. Every region a `setvar region_paddr` names fixes phys_addr, and
         none of those may stop doing so: a driver hands the address to its
         device, and the Microkit tool refuses that pairing without a fixed
         address — "setvar with 'region_paddr' for MR without a specified paddr
         is unsupported on x86-64", because the x86-64 physical memory layout is
         not known at build time (Microkit 2.3.0 manual, SDF `setvar`). Microkit
         therefore cannot place those regions for us; their addresses are chosen
         here, so what makes each one correct belongs here too.

         Two opposite requirements, both satisfied today by one unstated
         quantity — the QEMU memory size:

         * vq0..vq2 and the twelve pipeline regions
           (0x30000000..0x3108C000) must
           lie INSIDE RAM. They are ordinary memory, and seL4 zeroes a frame
           retyped from a general-purpose untyped but not one retyped from a
           device untyped — which is what an address outside RAM yields. That
           kernel behaviour is the whole of what establishes the
           zero-initialised precondition `virtio::queue::SplitVirtqueue::new`
           and `pd_runtime::attach_region!` both require of their mapping; no
           first-party code re-checks it, and a region placed outside RAM would
           fail as unbacked reads at run time rather than as a build or boot
           error.
         * bar0..bar2 (0x50000000..0x5000C000) must lie OUTSIDE RAM, in the q35
           32-bit PCI hole: each driver reprograms its device's 64-bit MMIO BAR
           to that address, so RAM underneath it would be shadowed by the
           device's decode.

         Under `-m 1G` (tools/xtask/src/qemu.rs) low RAM is [0, 0x40000000),
         which puts the first group inside it and the second above it. The
         window either side is narrow and nothing checks it: RAM must now reach
         past 0x3108C000 — 784.55 MiB rather than the 784.27 MiB the dataplane
         alone needed, so the management pipelines NARROW the lower end of that
         unasserted window by 280 KiB and every further port narrows it again —
         while at 1280 MiB or more RAM reaches 0x50000000 and swallows the BAR
         window. Changing `-m`, or targeting hardware with a different memory
         map, means re-deriving this map. README's status records the window as
         load-bearing and unasserted, and this is the change that made it
         tighter rather than the change that closed it.

         The two configuration regions (cfg, cfgack) are the exception and are
         deliberately NOT in the packed range, which now ends at 0x3108C000.
         No device DMAs into them, so no driver needs their physical address, so
         no `setvar region_paddr` names them and the tool has no reason to
         demand a fixed one. Leaving it out is not a convenience: an unplaced
         region is one Microkit allocates from general-purpose untyped memory,
         which is precisely the retyping seL4 zeroes — so `attach_region!`'s
         zero-initialisation precondition holds by construction for these two,
         rather than by the RAM-membership argument above, which rests on `-m`
         and which nothing checks. Fewer fixed addresses is the stronger
         position here, and the only reason the regions above keep theirs is
         that a device has to be told where they are.
         =================================================================== -->

    <!-- PCI ECAM pages for the pinned devices 00:02.0, 00:03.0 and 00:04.0 —
         the two dataplane ports and the management port. The deployable image
         boots through OVMF, which programs the q35 PCIEXBAR (MMCONFIG base) to
         0xE0000000; the page of device D is at base + (D << 15), which is what
         makes these three addresses a derivation rather than three choices.
         Device MMIO: uncached. -->
    <memory_region name="ecam0" size="0x1000" phys_addr="0xE0010000" />
    <memory_region name="ecam1" size="0x1000" phys_addr="0xE0018000" />
    <memory_region name="ecam2" size="0x1000" phys_addr="0xE0020000" />
    <!-- Where each driver relocates its device's modern MMIO BAR (patched into
         the driver via region_paddr). Packed back to back from the base of the
         q35 32-bit PCI hole, so the three together are 0x50000000..0x5000C000.
         Device MMIO: uncached. -->
    <memory_region name="bar0" size="0x4000" phys_addr="0x50000000" />
    <memory_region name="bar1" size="0x4000" phys_addr="0x50004000" />
    <memory_region name="bar2" size="0x4000" phys_addr="0x50008000" />

    <!-- The High Precision Event Timer's register block, and the third device
         region in this system.

         Its phys_addr is not ours to choose, unlike vq0/vq1 and the pipeline
         regions above: 0xFED00000 is where every x86 chipset places the block,
         and it is `lfw_hpet::MMIO_BASE`, which that crate forms every register
         address inside. The authoritative source is the ACPI HPET description
         table — and it is unusable here for the reason CONCEPT §12.3 fixes
         hardware at build time: a base discovered at run time is one no
         statically granted capability could map, so the conventional answer is
         the one that can be checked and the authoritative one is the one that
         cannot.

         SIZE IS A PAGE AND THE BLOCK IS 1 KIB, which is not slack anybody
         chose. A grant is whole pages, so `lfw_hpet::MMIO_REGION_SIZE` is
         `MMIO_LENGTH` rounded up to one and that is what `xtask::sysdesc`
         compares this row against — the same derivation `wire` makes for a log
         region. What keeps the rounding from widening the grant is a
         const-assertion in that crate: 0xFED00000 is page-aligned and the block
         is 0x400 bytes, so the whole of the page beyond the block is the same
         device's decode and no other device sits in it.

         Device MMIO: uncached, on the same footing as the ECAM pages and BAR
         windows above. A counter read through a cached mapping is read once and
         then answered from the cache forever, which is a stopped clock that
         reports itself running — the one failure `lfw_hpet` cannot detect,
         since it distinguishes a stalled counter from a slow one by reading it
         again. -->
    <memory_region name="hpet" size="0x1000" phys_addr="0xFED00000" />

    <!-- virtio virtqueues (driver <-> NIC), receive and transmit per port.

         Every map of a virtqueue or a pipeline below states cached="true"
         rather than leaning on the default, because both are DMA memory and
         `virtio::queue` names a cached mapping as a premise of its correctness
         argument: its fences order CPU-visible memory only, which suffices
         because x86 DMA is cache-coherent and the region is cached. A premise a
         crate reasons from is declared where the mapping is granted, not
         inherited from a default that can move. -->
    <memory_region name="vq0" size="0x1000" phys_addr="0x30000000" />
    <memory_region name="vq1" size="0x1000" phys_addr="0x30001000" />
    <memory_region name="vq2" size="0x1000" phys_addr="0x30002000" />

    <!-- Three regions per forwarding direction (rx driver -> forwarder -> tx
         driver), because a region is the unit of grant: what a domain can reach
         is decided here and not by which fields its code touches. pipe 0 is
         port 0 -> port 1; pipe 1 the reverse.

         Each pool is both NICs' DMA target, so it needs a fixed physical
         address the drivers hand to their devices (region_paddr, below).

         Every size is a `pd_runtime` constant rather than a chosen number:
         `POOL_REGION_SIZE`, `FORWARD_REGION_SIZE` and `RETURN_REGION_SIZE`, each
         its region type's `size_of` rounded up to the mapping granularity. It is
         one value in two places, and `xtask::sysdesc` reads this file back and
         fails the gate on any inequality: moving one side alone would otherwise
         silently truncate the mapping or re-widen the grant.

         Splitting the pool from the rings costs one page. `ForwardRings` is
         4112 bytes and `ReturnRing` 2056; the first needs two pages and the
         second one, so 0x20000 + 0x2000 + 0x1000 is one page more than the
         0x22000 a single region holding all four components rounds up to.
         `pd_runtime` asserts that equality at build time, and that page is what
         keeps the `free` ring out of the forwarder's address space entirely.

         The regions stay packed back to back: 0x31000000 through 0x31046000. -->
    <memory_region name="pool0" size="0x20000" phys_addr="0x31000000" />
    <memory_region name="fwd0" size="0x2000" phys_addr="0x31020000" />
    <memory_region name="free0" size="0x1000" phys_addr="0x31022000" />
    <memory_region name="pool1" size="0x20000" phys_addr="0x31023000" />
    <memory_region name="fwd1" size="0x2000" phys_addr="0x31043000" />
    <memory_region name="free1" size="0x1000" phys_addr="0x31045000" />

    <!-- The MANAGEMENT PORT'S two pipelines, the same three regions each and the
         same `pd_runtime` constants, packed immediately after the dataplane's:
         0x31046000 through 0x3108C000.

         BOTH PIPELINES ARE NOW USED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS, which is the change this
         port's addressing brought (ENG-1, SCM-6). The receive three carry frames
         to the management domain as before; the transmit three now carry the
         replies that domain originates, so the driver binary's transmit side —
         which existed because the binary is port-agnostic and takes one whatever
         port it drives (pds/nic-driver's crate header) — has a producer at last.

         What still differs from the dataplane is the far end of the receive
         pipeline. There is no egress driver to hand a descriptor onward to, so
         the management domain is the end of the journey and produces the return
         itself onto mgmt_rx_free — the same producer/consumer split the dataplane
         already has between its two drivers, with that domain standing where the
         egress driver stands.

         THE TWO POOLS ARE OWNED BY DIFFERENT DOMAINS, in opposite directions,
         and that asymmetry is the whole of why a forged return is refused on
         both. mgmt_rx_pool is the DRIVER's: it receives into it, and the
         management domain maps it READ-ONLY to copy a frame out — a frame this
         appliance was sent is never altered, so read is the whole of the
         authority that reading it needs. mgmt_tx_pool is the MANAGEMENT
         DOMAIN's: it allocates a buffer, writes a reply into it (hence
         read-write) and lends it, and the driver maps it because `TxPath::post`
         writes the 12-byte virtio-net header in front of the frame.

         mgmt_rx_pool was mapped by no protection domain at all until this
         change, and that claim is gone rather than weakened: the grant arrived
         with the code that reads a frame, which is exactly the condition the
         note it replaces set for it (ENG-7). `xtask::sysdesc` carries the new
         mapper set as a rule, read-only side included, so a widening to
         read-write fails the gate at the point the edit is made. -->
    <memory_region name="mgmt_rx_pool" size="0x20000" phys_addr="0x31046000" />
    <memory_region name="mgmt_rx_fwd" size="0x2000" phys_addr="0x31066000" />
    <memory_region name="mgmt_rx_free" size="0x1000" phys_addr="0x31068000" />
    <memory_region name="mgmt_tx_pool" size="0x20000" phys_addr="0x31069000" />
    <memory_region name="mgmt_tx_fwd" size="0x2000" phys_addr="0x31089000" />
    <memory_region name="mgmt_tx_free" size="0x1000" phys_addr="0x3108B000" />

    <!-- The configuration handover, one region per direction.

         `cfg` carries the validated configuration image and the two generation
         words that publish it; `cfgack` carries what the one consumer has done
         with the offered generation. They are two regions rather than one field
         set for the reason the pipeline is three regions: a region is the unit
         of grant, and only two regions can give each domain write access to
         exactly the direction it speaks in. The config domain cannot forge an
         acknowledgement to release its own generation, and the forwarder cannot
         rewrite the configuration it is about to be judged by.

         `cfg` HAS TWO READERS AND `cfgack` STILL ONE WRITER, and the asymmetry
         is a decision rather than an omission (ENG-1, SCM-6). The management
         domain maps `cfg` read-only — its addressing comes from the document
         (CONCEPT §12.3) — and maps `cfgack` NOT AT ALL, because it is not the
         *consumer* of the two-phase commit. The forwarder is: it reads the
         offered generation, stages a table and acknowledges, and a commit waits
         for that. The management domain reads the COMMITTED generation only
         (`pd_runtime::CommittedReader`), which is a strictly weaker role — it
         cannot delay a commit, cannot refuse one on anybody's behalf, and holds
         no region to forge an acknowledgement in. Granting it `cfgack` would
         make "every consumer has staged" a claim about two domains and hand a
         domain that answers the management-plane attacker the word that releases
         a generation.

         What that costs is stated at pds/management: with no channel to the
         config domain, that port picks up its address on the next frame that
         wakes it rather than at the commit.

         Sizes are `wire::CONFIG_REGION_SIZE` and `wire::CONFIG_ACK_REGION_SIZE`,
         each its type's `size_of` rounded up to the mapping granularity, on the
         same footing as the pipeline constants above and held to them the same
         way: `xtask::sysdesc` carries a rule for each of these two regions and
         compares it. `ConfigHandover` is 664 bytes and `ConfigAck` 8, so both
         round to one page and the pair costs two.

         Neither carries phys_addr; see the address-map block above on why that
         is the stronger position for a region no device is told about. -->
    <memory_region name="cfg" size="0x1000" />
    <memory_region name="cfgack" size="0x1000" />

    <!-- THE CLOCK CALIBRATION, and the first region in this system published
         under a SEQLOCK rather than under an identifying generation
         (ENG-1, SCM-6).

         It carries three words — the timestamp counter's measured frequency, the
         reading it was anchored on, and the wall-clock instant that reading
         corresponds to — and they are only meaningful TOGETHER: a frequency from
         one measurement anchored to another measurement's epoch names an instant
         that never existed. Every bit pattern of each of the three is
         individually plausible, so a torn triple is undetectable by inspection,
         which is exactly what `cfg`'s "publish the bytes, then release the
         generation" pattern relies on not being the case. `wire::ClockCalibration`
         therefore spends its counter on the write rather than on the identity —
         even settled, odd being written — and a reader that cannot get a matching
         pair of even readings answers "nothing" rather than assembling a triple
         from two publishes.

         ONE WRITER AND ONE READER, and the direction is the whole grant. The
         clock domain maps it READ-WRITE: it is the domain that measured the
         numbers, and it publishes them once, after the record that says what it
         measured. The management domain maps it READ-ONLY, on the same footing as
         `cfg`: it is a CONSUMER of what was measured, and a domain that could
         write it would be able to move this node's own idea of time — every
         retransmission deadline and every reaping deadline of the transport on
         its port, and one day every certificate's validity window (CONCEPT §7.2)
         — from the domain that answers the management-plane attacker.

         WHAT STAYS WITHHELD: every other domain. The forwarder, the configuration
         domain, the three drivers and the console map no part of it, in either
         direction. None of them states a time and none reads one, so a mapping
         would be authority with no use (ENG-1) — and the console in particular
         must not be able to rewrite the frequency it is printing.

         WHY THE MANAGEMENT DOMAIN READS A REGION RATHER THAN ASKING. There is no
         IPC for a timestamp: that domain reads `RDTSC` itself, which is one
         unprivileged instruction, and converts it with the triple published here.
         A channel to the clock domain would put a round trip on the path of every
         frame and would give this domain a wakeup capability on a domain that
         runs once and parks. What that costs is stated at pds/management: with no
         channel, a republished calibration is picked up on the next frame.

         Size is `wire::CLOCK_CALIBRATION_REGION_SIZE`, its type's `size_of`
         rounded up to the mapping granularity, on the same footing as every
         constant above and held to it the same way — `xtask::sysdesc` carries a
         rule for this region and compares it. `ClockCalibration` is 32 bytes, so
         it rounds to one page.

         No phys_addr, for the reason the address-map block above gives for `cfg`:
         no device DMAs into it, so no domain needs its physical address, and an
         unplaced region is allocated from general-purpose untyped memory — which
         is precisely the retyping seL4 zeroes, so `attach_region!`'s
         zero-initialisation precondition holds by construction. A zeroed region
         is generation zero, which `load` refuses, so a management domain that
         comes up before the clock domain has published reads "no calibration"
         rather than a triple of zeroes. -->
    <memory_region name="clock" size="0x1000" />

    <!-- The log transport: one ring per WRITING domain, and TWO REGIONS PER
         RING, one for each direction.

         The console is a device with one owner (see the console domain below),
         so every other domain reaches it by publishing a typed record into a
         ring of its own and letting that domain render it. One ring per writer
         rather than one shared ring is the same argument the pipeline and the
         configuration handover make, sharpened by what a single writer buys
         here: the ring is single-producer/single-consumer, so one writer per
         ring makes a torn record — two domains' bytes interleaved into one
         console line — unrepresentable rather than prevented by scheduling. It
         is also the only shape the ring protocol admits at all; a second
         producer on one of these is two writers racing for the same slot.

         THE SPLIT EXISTS SO THE TWO DIRECTIONS CARRY OPPOSITE PERMISSIONS, and
         that is the whole reason there are fourteen rows here rather than seven. A
         region is the unit of grant, so only two regions can give each domain
         write access to exactly the direction it speaks in — the same reason
         `cfg`/`cfgack` are two regions and each dataplane pipeline is three.

         `log_<domain>` carries the slots, the producer cursor and the writer's
         drop count. The writing domain maps it READ-WRITE and the console maps
         it READ-ONLY, so THE CONSOLE CANNOT FORGE A RECORD INTO A DOMAIN'S
         RING: it cannot store into a slot, cannot advance the cursor that
         publishes one, and cannot rewrite the count of what that domain says it
         lost. A console that could would be able to attribute a line to a
         domain that never emitted it — and it is the one domain in the system
         whose output an operator reads as testimony about the others.

         `log_<domain>_consume` carries the console's consume cursor and nothing
         else. The console maps it READ-WRITE and the writing domain maps it
         READ-ONLY, so A WRITER CANNOT FORGE THE CURSOR THAT SAYS HOW MUCH OF
         ITS RING HAS BEEN READ. A writer that could would move it forward to
         reuse slots the console has not rendered, discarding records while its
         own drop count stayed at zero — silent loss reported as none, which is
         worse than loss.

         A single region could carry neither property. `wire`'s
         `LogReader::read` publishes its consume position on every record it
         takes (crates/wire/src/log_ring.rs), and that word is the writer's only
         flow control: mapped read-only the console would fault on the first
         record it consumed, and mapped read-write it would hold write access to
         every slot in the ring. Two regions is what removes the choice between
         those two.

         What the rows isolate BETWEEN writers is unchanged and still worth
         stating: no writer maps another writer's region in either direction, so
         a compromised parser domain cannot read what a driver has said about
         itself, cannot rewrite it, and cannot silence it. Every pair of writing
         domains is disjoint here, and that is enforced by these rows and by
         `xtask::sysdesc`'s per-mapper grant set.

         The residue, named rather than left to be discovered: the console still
         decides what reaches the line, so it can decline to print any record
         and can emit any line it likes as itself — it owns the port and is the
         sole renderer. What it can no longer do is put words in another
         domain's mouth, or make that domain lose records it will report as
         delivered.

         Sizes are `wire::LOG_RECORDS_REGION_SIZE` and
         `wire::LOG_CONSUME_REGION_SIZE`, each its type's `size_of` rounded up
         to the mapping granularity, on the same footing as every constant above
         and held to it the same way — `xtask::sysdesc` carries a rule per
         region and compares it. `LogRecords` is 13320 bytes (64 slots of a
         208-byte record, plus the producer cursor and the drop count) so each
         is four pages; `LogConsume` is one word and so costs the page a region
         costs, exactly as `cfgack` does. The ten together are 100 KiB.

         None carries phys_addr, for the reason the address-map block above
         gives for `cfg` and `cfgack`: no device DMAs into a log region, so no
         domain needs its physical address, so the tool has no reason to demand
         a fixed one — and an unplaced region is allocated from general-purpose
         untyped memory, which is precisely the retyping seL4 zeroes. That is
         what makes `attach_region!`'s zero-initialisation precondition hold by
         construction here, and it is also what makes the boot transcript work:
         a zeroed pair is a valid empty ring, so a domain may publish records
         during its own `init`, before the console domain has run at all, and
         they are still there when it comes up. -->
    <memory_region name="log_forwarder" size="0x4000" />
    <memory_region name="log_forwarder_consume" size="0x1000" />
    <memory_region name="log_nic_driver0" size="0x4000" />
    <memory_region name="log_nic_driver0_consume" size="0x1000" />
    <memory_region name="log_nic_driver1" size="0x4000" />
    <memory_region name="log_nic_driver1_consume" size="0x1000" />
    <memory_region name="log_config" size="0x4000" />
    <memory_region name="log_config_consume" size="0x1000" />
    <memory_region name="log_clock" size="0x4000" />
    <memory_region name="log_clock_consume" size="0x1000" />
    <memory_region name="log_nic_driver2" size="0x4000" />
    <memory_region name="log_nic_driver2_consume" size="0x1000" />
    <memory_region name="log_management" size="0x4000" />
    <memory_region name="log_management_consume" size="0x1000" />


    <!-- THE METRIC SHARDS: one region per protection domain, each written by
         that domain alone and read by the management domain alone
         (ENG-1, SCM-6).

         WHY EIGHT REGIONS AND NOT ONE. A region is the unit of grant, and the
         property worth having here is that a domain's numbers are *its own
         testimony*: the management domain renders every one of them into the
         exposition an operator scrapes, so it must read all eight, and a single
         shared region would make it a writer of all eight too. It could then
         attribute a driver's dropped frames to another driver, or zero a count
         that accuses itself — and it is the domain that faces the
         management-plane attacker. Eight regions with eight writers is what
         makes each series a claim only its own domain can make, and it is the
         same argument the fourteen log regions make one step further: there the
         console cannot forge a *record*, here it cannot forge a *number*.

         PER-DOMAIN SERIES, NO TOTALS. Nothing in this node sums across shards.
         A summed total is corrupted by a domain restart — one shard goes back to
         zero, the sum goes backwards, and a scraper differencing two samples
         forges an enormous rate — whereas a `domain=`-labelled series resets
         alone, which Prometheus handles by design. MONITORING.md records the
         decision; this row set is what makes it possible.

         SINGLE WRITER IS THE WHOLE SYNCHRONISATION. Exactly one domain has each
         region read-write, so an increment is a relaxed 64-bit load, an add and
         a relaxed store — no `lock` prefix and no contention — and on x86_64 an
         aligned 64-bit access cannot tear, so the reader never sees half a
         value. Unlike `clock` this needs no seqlock: a calibration's three words
         are meaningful only together and a counter is a number in its own right
         (`lfw_metrics`, crate header).

         WHAT STAYS WITHHELD: every domain but the writer and the reader, in
         either direction. A driver maps no other driver's shard, the forwarder
         maps none of them but its own, and the console — which renders an
         operator's only other output — maps none at all. A domain that could
         write another's shard could make a port that is dropping every frame
         report a clean line.

         AND THE READER CANNOT WRITE. Every grant to `management` below is
         READ-ONLY except its own. That is the same asymmetry `cfg` and `clock`
         have, and for a sharper reason: this domain is the one an attacker
         reaches first, and a `/metrics` surface it could edit would let a
         compromise of it hide the compromise.

         `stats_management` HAS EXACTLY ONE MAPPER, and that is not an oversight.
         The management domain both writes and reads it, because the renderer
         walks one uniform array of eight shards rather than seven regions plus a
         live read of its own counters — one code path, and a set of numbers
         taken at one publish rather than half of them changing under the render.
         It costs no cross-domain authority: no other domain maps it.

         Size is `lfw_metrics::STATS_REGION_SIZE`, the type's `size_of` rounded
         up to the mapping granularity, on the same footing as every constant
         above and held to it the same way — `xtask::sysdesc` carries a rule per
         region and compares it. A `StatsShard` is 768 bytes, so each rounds to
         one page and the eight cost 32 KiB.

         None carries phys_addr, for the reason the address-map block above gives
         for `cfg`: no device DMAs into a shard, so no domain needs its physical
         address, and an unplaced region is allocated from general-purpose
         untyped memory — precisely the retyping seL4 zeroes. A zeroed shard is
         every counter at zero, which is the state a domain that has done nothing
         is in, so a scrape taken before a domain has published reads zeroes
         rather than rubbish. -->
    <memory_region name="stats_forwarder" size="0x1000" />
    <memory_region name="stats_nic_driver0" size="0x1000" />
    <memory_region name="stats_nic_driver1" size="0x1000" />
    <memory_region name="stats_nic_driver2" size="0x1000" />
    <memory_region name="stats_management" size="0x1000" />
    <memory_region name="stats_console" size="0x1000" />
    <memory_region name="stats_config" size="0x1000" />
    <memory_region name="stats_clock" size="0x1000" />

    <!-- The forwarder maps four dataplane regions and two configuration ones,
         and holds no device capability at all. IT IS GRANTED NO MANAGEMENT
         REGION, in either direction and of either pipeline, and the management
         domain below is granted no dataplane region — the mutual exclusion
         CONCEPT §9.1 requires of a management port that carries no forwarded
         traffic. Neither half is a comment: `xtask::sysdesc` names the mapper
         set of every region exactly, so either grant appearing fails the gate at
         the point the edit is made (ENG-1, SCM-6).
         `RouteStage` decides on each frame and rewrites its Ethernet and IPv4
         headers in the buffer the receiving NIC DMA'd it into, so it is granted
         the two pools as well as the two ring pairs: a domain that rewrites a
         header must reach the bytes, and that authority is the price of routing
         in place rather than copying every frame between two pools.

         What stays withheld is the `free` ring of either pipeline, and it
         matters more than the pool ever did. A forged return puts a live DMA
         target back onto an owner's free stack, to be handed out a second time
         while the first NIC is still writing it; and the forwarder cannot
         produce onto that ring in any case without being a second producer on
         an SPSC ring that admits exactly one. Corrupting a frame in flight —
         which the pool grant does permit — damages one packet, inside a region
         both drivers already share.

         272 KiB mapped: the same byte count as the two undivided pipeline
         regions this replaced, and deliberately not the same bytes. What the
         split buys is no longer a smaller grant but a different one.

         It additionally maps the two configuration regions, and the perms are
         the whole of the argument. `cfg` is READ-ONLY here: the forwarder is
         the domain that decides under the configuration, so it must read it,
         and a domain that could also write it could rewrite the table it is
         about to be held to and leave the publishing domain reporting a
         generation nobody is running. `cfgack` is read-write because
         acknowledging is this domain's own statement about itself — what it has
         staged and what it is running — and it is the only domain that may make
         it. The two perms together are what make the handover a protocol
         between two domains rather than a shared scratch area. -->
    <protection_domain name="forwarder" priority="2" stack_size="0x4000">
        <program_image path="forwarder.elf" />
        <map mr="fwd0" vaddr="0x2_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="fwd0_vaddr" />
        <map mr="fwd1" vaddr="0x2_100_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="fwd1_vaddr" />
        <map mr="pool0" vaddr="0x2_200_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="pool0_vaddr" />
        <map mr="pool1" vaddr="0x2_300_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="pool1_vaddr" />
        <map mr="cfg" vaddr="0x3_000_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="cfg_vaddr" />
        <map mr="cfgack" vaddr="0x3_001_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="cfgack_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_forwarder" vaddr="0x4_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_records_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_forwarder_consume" vaddr="0x4_010_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_consume_vaddr" />
        <map mr="stats_forwarder" vaddr="0x5_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="stats_vaddr" />
    </protection_domain>

    <!-- The configuration domain: it reads the document compiled into it,
         refuses or commits it, and publishes the result. Its whole grant is the
         two regions above, mirrored — `cfg` read-write because it is the writer
         of the configuration, `cfgack` read-only because the acknowledgement is
         the consumer's statement and a publisher that could forge one would
         release a generation nobody had staged, which is the entire point of
         there being two phases.

         It holds NO device capability, NO buffer pool and NO dataplane ring, so
         the domain that parses attacker-supplied XML cannot reach a frame, a
         NIC, or the memory either travels through. That is the reason this is a
         domain of its own rather than a function in the forwarder.

         Priority 3, above the forwarder's 2 and the drivers' 1, and the
         ordering is load-bearing in one direction only. The drivers never leave
         `init` — they busy-loop at priority 1 — so a configuration domain at or
         below their priority would be starved of the CPU it needs to publish
         anything at all, and the appliance would come up permanently on
         generation 0. Above them it runs to completion first and then blocks in
         the Microkit event loop, where it costs nothing: it has no polling loop
         and no work except when the consumer acknowledges, so being the highest
         priority in the system cannot starve the dataplane.

         cached="true" on both, as everywhere: they are ordinary memory shared
         between two domains and no device decodes them. -->
    <protection_domain name="config" priority="3" stack_size="0x10000">
        <program_image path="config-pd.elf" />
        <map mr="cfg" vaddr="0x3_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="cfg_vaddr" />
        <map mr="cfgack" vaddr="0x3_001_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="cfgack_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_config" vaddr="0x4_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_records_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_config_consume" vaddr="0x4_010_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_consume_vaddr" />
        <map mr="stats_config" vaddr="0x5_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="stats_vaddr" />
    </protection_domain>

    <!-- The clock domain: it establishes what time it is, once, and says so.

         It maps the HPET page, holds an I/O-port capability for the CMOS
         real-time clock, calibrates the timestamp counter against the timer
         whose rate is self-describing, reads the RTC once for an epoch to
         anchor that counter to, publishes one record and parks. The protection
         domain is documented in pds/clock.

         =================================================================
         `<ioport>` IS NO LONGER A CAPABILITY CLASS ONE DOMAIN HOLDS ALONE
         (ENG-1, SCM-6)
         =================================================================

         READ THE CONSOLE DOMAIN'S `<ioport>` BLOCK BELOW BEFORE THIS ONE. The
         lesson it records was paid for once and applies unchanged here: the
         element places an X86IOPort CAPABILITY in this domain's CNode and does
         NOT unlock an instruction. seL4 leaves the TSS I/O permission bitmap
         denying every port and never edits it, so `in` and `out` executed in
         this domain raise #GP with this grant present and correct; the ports
         below are reached only by invoking the capability through
         `seL4_X86_IOPort_In8` / `seL4_X86_IOPort_Out8`, which is what
         `pds/clock/src/cmos.rs` does and why that file needs no `unsafe`.

         WHAT IT GRANTS: two consecutive I/O ports, 0x70 and 0x71 — the CMOS
         address and data registers, `lfw_rtc::INDEX_PORT` and `DATA_PORT`. That
         crate writes an index to the first and reads the answer from the
         second, and the nine registers it can name are `Register::ALL`, every
         one of them const-asserted to carry bit 7 clear — so no index it can
         form disables the non-maskable interrupt, and no address it can form
         leaves this row.

         WHAT STAYS WITHHELD is everything the console's block already
         enumerates, minus the CMOS pair this row now grants: no PCI
         configuration address/data pair at 0xCF8/0xCFC, no PS/2 controller, no
         PIC, no PIT, no debug port, and no serial controller — this domain
         cannot write the console it publishes through, which is the whole point
         of publishing through a ring. The drivers and the forwarder still hold
         zero I/O ports between them.

         What the *pair* of grants costs, stated because it is the property that
         changed: the console's claim to be the system's only port holder is
         gone, and with it the argument that an attacker reaching any
         non-console domain reaches no port instruction the kernel will accept.
         What survives is narrower and still worth having — the two windows are
         disjoint, each is held by exactly one domain, and neither domain can
         reach the other's device. In particular this domain cannot splice bytes
         into a console line, and the console cannot read or stop the clock.

         WHY THE CMOS IS A DOMAIN OF ITS OWN rather than a function in the
         console, which already holds a port capability: the two devices are
         unrelated, and merging them would hand the domain that drives the
         operator's only output channel a second device — and hand the domain
         that reads a battery-backed register file the ability to write the line
         an operator reads its result on. The split is what makes "the console
         renders what the clock said" a statement about a ring rather than about
         one domain's own bookkeeping.

         PRIORITY 3, EQUAL TO THE CONFIGURATION DOMAIN, and the reasoning is
         that domain's, one step further. The drivers and the console busy-loop
         forever at priority 1 and the forwarder runs at 2, so a domain that
         must run once and publish cannot sit at or below them: it would depend
         on seL4's round-robin against three domains that never yield, for the
         whole of a calibration window it is trying to measure real time across.
         Above them it runs to completion and then blocks in the Microkit event
         loop, where it costs nothing at all — it holds no channel, so nothing
         can ever wake it again.

         Equal to the configuration domain rather than above it, because both
         are run-once-then-block domains and neither can starve the other: each
         leaves `init` for good. Adding a fourth priority level would state an
         ordering between the two that nothing needs — no record either writes
         depends on the other, and the console renders both whenever it gets to
         them.

         What that priority does cost, named rather than left to be measured: a
         calibration window is about a millisecond of real time during which
         this domain preempts the dataplane. It happens once, at boot, before
         any traffic exists.

         It maps NO pool, NO dataplane ring, NO configuration region, NO ECAM
         page and NO BAR window, so a compromised clock reaches no frame, no NIC
         and no configuration — the shape of isolation the configuration and
         console domains already have, in a third direction. What it DOES map, and
         the only region it shares with anybody, is `clock` (read-write): the
         calibration it publishes, read by the management domain alone. See the
         note beside that region. -->
    <protection_domain name="clock" priority="3" stack_size="0x4000">
        <program_image path="clock.elf" />
        <ioport id="0" addr="0x70" size="2" />
        <map mr="hpet" vaddr="0x10_000_000" perms="rw" cached="false" setvar_vaddr="hpet_vaddr" />
        <map mr="clock" vaddr="0x3_002_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="clock_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_clock" vaddr="0x4_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_records_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_clock_consume" vaddr="0x4_010_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_consume_vaddr" />
        <map mr="stats_clock" vaddr="0x5_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="stats_vaddr" />
    </protection_domain>

    <!-- The management domain: the ADDRESSED endpoint of the management port. It
         answers ARP requests for its own address, ICMP echo requests to it, and
         TCP connections to its one listening port; it counts what the port
         receives, and forwards nothing — nothing in this description gives it the
         means to.

         IT NOW READS TWO INSTRUCTIONS AND STILL HOLDS NO CAPABILITY FOR EITHER.
         `RDTSC` once per wakeup, for the instant its transport's timers are
         stated against, and `RDRAND` once at start-up, for the secret those
         connections\' initial sequence numbers are derived from (RFC 6528). Both
         are unprivileged instructions rather than capabilities — no `<ioport>`,
         no `<irq>`, no mapping — so nothing in this file grants or could withhold
         them, and a part with no `RDRAND` refuses this domain rather than
         answering it with a predictable sequence number. That refusal is a
         console record, and pds/management is where the argument lives.

         =================================================================
         WHY THIS DOMAIN IS THE RETURNER ON ONE PIPELINE AND THE OWNER ON
         THE OTHER (ENG-1, SCM-6)
         =================================================================

         The forwarder maps NEITHER dataplane `free` ring, and the note beside
         those regions says why: a forged return puts a live DMA target back onto
         an owner's free stack to be handed out a second time while a NIC is
         still writing it. That withholding is affordable there because a
         dataplane descriptor travels ONWARD — the egress driver is what returns
         the buffer, and it is the only domain that can.

         A terminal port has no egress driver, so that argument does not
         transfer, and the choice is not between granting the ring and
         withholding it. It is between granting it and leaking the pool: a
         consumed buffer with no path back is a buffer gone from the driver's
         ledger for good, and 64 frames later the port stops receiving. So
         mgmt_rx_free is granted READ-WRITE here, and what keeps that from being
         the authority the forwarder was refused is WHICH SIDE OF THE RING it is:
         this domain PRODUCES returns and nic_driver2 CONSUMES them as the pool's
         owner (`pd_runtime::PoolOwner::reclaim`), which accepts an index only if
         it is in that driver's own lent set and its ledger agrees. A return it
         forges names a buffer the driver never lent and is refused and counted
         there.

         ON THE TRANSMIT PIPELINE THE ROLES ARE EXACTLY REVERSED. A reply is a
         frame this domain originates, so it is the OWNER of mgmt_tx_pool: it
         allocates a buffer, writes the reply into it, lends it on mgmt_tx_fwd,
         and CONSUMES the returns nic_driver2 produces onto mgmt_tx_free. So each
         pool has one owner, each owner holds the ledger for its own pool, and
         each `free` ring has one producer and one consumer — which is what makes
         a forged return refused rather than believed in both directions. The
         dataplane already has this shape between its two drivers; here one domain
         stands at each end.

         WHAT IT MAPS, and why each grant exists:

           * mgmt_rx_fwd (rw) — consume the descriptors the driver publishes.
           * mgmt_rx_free (rw) — produce the returns, per the argument above.
           * mgmt_rx_pool (READ-ONLY) — copy the received frame out to parse it.
             Read is the whole of the authority parsing needs: a frame this
             appliance was sent is never altered, and the reply is composed
             elsewhere. This is the grant the previous note deferred to "the day
             this port parses ARP or IP", and it arrived with that code (ENG-7).
           * mgmt_tx_pool (rw) — write the reply into the buffer it owns.
           * mgmt_tx_fwd (rw) — lend the reply to the driver.
           * mgmt_tx_free (rw) — consume the driver's returns as the pool's owner.
           * cfg (READ-ONLY) — the addressing comes from the configuration
             document (CONCEPT §12.3). Read-only because a domain that could
             write it would rewrite the addressing it is about to be judged by,
             and because the publisher must be able to read back what it wrote.
           * clock (READ-ONLY) — what a counter reading means. Every timer of the
             transport on this port is stated against an instant, and an instant
             is a `RDTSC` reading converted by the triple the clock domain
             published. Read-only because a domain that could write it would move
             this node's own idea of time from the one domain that answers the
             management-plane attacker; see the note beside that region.

         WHAT STAYS WITHHELD, and it is the whole of the isolation CONCEPT §9.1
         asks for: NO dataplane region of any kind — not a pool, not a
         `ForwardRings`, not a return ring — NO ECAM page, NO BAR window, NO
         virtqueue and NO ioport. A compromise of the domain that will one day
         terminate an operator's TLS session therefore reaches no frame in flight
         and no NIC.

         AND NO `cfgack`, WHICH IS THE ONE WORTH DWELLING ON. This domain is not
         the consumer of the two-phase commit: it reads the COMMITTED generation
         alone (`pd_runtime::CommittedReader`), so it cannot delay a commit,
         cannot refuse one on anybody's behalf, and holds no region an
         acknowledgement could be forged in. See the note beside cfg/cfgack.

         PRIORITY 2, EQUAL TO THE FORWARDER, and for the forwarder's reason: this
         is a `notified`-driven domain among three drivers that busy-loop forever
         at priority 1, so at or below them a wakeup would wait on seL4's
         round-robin against domains that never yield. Above them it preempts on
         notification, drains, and returns to the event loop. Equal to the
         forwarder rather than above or below it because neither can starve the
         other — each runs to completion per wakeup — and an ordering between the
         two would be a claim nothing needs: no frame crosses from one to the
         other, which is the point of the port.

         STACK 0x40000, and it is the HTTP server that decides it. This domain
         holds, in the value `init` returns and therefore on its stack: two
         `BUFFER_SIZE` scratch buffers (the frame snapshotted out of the pool and
         the reply composed into storage of its own, the pool exposing no safe
         path to its bytes), a request slot of `lfw_http::MAX_REQUEST_BYTES` per
         connection, and one `lfw_ip_endpoint::http::RESPONSE_CAPACITY` staging
         buffer — about 29 KiB, because a Prometheus exposition of this node's
         whole metric catalogue is that big and a response composed anywhere else
         could not be retransmitted. That is roughly 50 KiB live, and `init`
         moves the endpoint into place once, so the peak is about twice it. A
         quarter of a mebibyte leaves the margin an unmeasured peak needs; it is
         memory rather than authority, and no other domain's stack moves. -->
    <protection_domain name="management" priority="2" stack_size="0x40000">
        <program_image path="management.elf" />
        <map mr="mgmt_rx_fwd" vaddr="0x2_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="mgmt_rx_fwd_vaddr" />
        <map mr="mgmt_rx_free" vaddr="0x2_002_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="mgmt_rx_free_vaddr" />
        <map mr="mgmt_rx_pool" vaddr="0x2_004_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="mgmt_rx_pool_vaddr" />
        <map mr="mgmt_tx_fwd" vaddr="0x2_100_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="mgmt_tx_fwd_vaddr" />
        <map mr="mgmt_tx_free" vaddr="0x2_102_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="mgmt_tx_free_vaddr" />
        <map mr="mgmt_tx_pool" vaddr="0x2_200_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="mgmt_tx_pool_vaddr" />
        <map mr="cfg" vaddr="0x3_000_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="cfg_vaddr" />
        <map mr="clock" vaddr="0x3_002_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="clock_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_management" vaddr="0x4_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_records_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_management_consume" vaddr="0x4_010_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_consume_vaddr" />
        <map mr="stats_forwarder" vaddr="0x5_000_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="stats_forwarder_vaddr" />
        <map mr="stats_nic_driver0" vaddr="0x5_001_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="stats_nic_driver0_vaddr" />
        <map mr="stats_nic_driver1" vaddr="0x5_002_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="stats_nic_driver1_vaddr" />
        <map mr="stats_nic_driver2" vaddr="0x5_003_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="stats_nic_driver2_vaddr" />
        <map mr="stats_management" vaddr="0x5_004_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="stats_management_vaddr" />
        <map mr="stats_console" vaddr="0x5_005_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="stats_console_vaddr" />
        <map mr="stats_config" vaddr="0x5_006_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="stats_config_vaddr" />
        <map mr="stats_clock" vaddr="0x5_007_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="stats_clock_vaddr" />
    </protection_domain>

    <!-- Each driver maps five of its six pipeline regions. The exception is the
         pool it receives INTO: it hands that pool's physical address to its NIC
         as a DMA target and never dereferences a byte of it, so it takes the
         `setvar region_paddr` with no `map` at all. Microkit 2.3.0 patches a
         `region_paddr` whether or not the same PD maps the region (verified
         against the tool, which accepts this description and still emits the
         region's frames); the only thing it refuses is a `region_paddr` for a
         memory region with no `phys_addr`, which every region above fixes.

         The pool a driver transmits OUT of is mapped, because `TxPath::post`
         writes the 12-byte virtio-net header in front of the frame — the only
         production dereference of pool bytes anywhere in the system. -->
    <protection_domain name="nic_driver0" priority="1" stack_size="0x8000">
        <program_image path="nic-driver.elf" />
        <map mr="ecam0" vaddr="0x10_000_000" perms="rw" cached="false" setvar_vaddr="ecam_vaddr" />
        <map mr="bar0" vaddr="0x10_100_000" perms="rw" cached="false" setvar_vaddr="bar_vaddr" />
        <map mr="vq0" vaddr="0x10_200_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="vq_vaddr" />
        <map mr="fwd0" vaddr="0x2_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="rx_fwd_vaddr" />
        <map mr="free0" vaddr="0x2_002_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="rx_free_vaddr" />
        <map mr="fwd1" vaddr="0x2_100_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="tx_fwd_vaddr" />
        <map mr="free1" vaddr="0x2_102_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="tx_free_vaddr" />
        <map mr="pool1" vaddr="0x2_200_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="tx_pool_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_nic_driver0" vaddr="0x4_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_records_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_nic_driver0_consume" vaddr="0x4_010_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_consume_vaddr" />
        <map mr="stats_nic_driver0" vaddr="0x5_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="stats_vaddr" />
        <setvar symbol="bar_paddr" region_paddr="bar0" />
        <setvar symbol="vq_paddr" region_paddr="vq0" />
        <setvar symbol="rx_pool_paddr" region_paddr="pool0" />
        <setvar symbol="tx_pool_paddr" region_paddr="pool1" />
    </protection_domain>

    <protection_domain name="nic_driver1" priority="1" stack_size="0x8000">
        <program_image path="nic-driver.elf" />
        <map mr="ecam1" vaddr="0x10_000_000" perms="rw" cached="false" setvar_vaddr="ecam_vaddr" />
        <map mr="bar1" vaddr="0x10_100_000" perms="rw" cached="false" setvar_vaddr="bar_vaddr" />
        <map mr="vq1" vaddr="0x10_200_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="vq_vaddr" />
        <map mr="fwd1" vaddr="0x2_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="rx_fwd_vaddr" />
        <map mr="free1" vaddr="0x2_002_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="rx_free_vaddr" />
        <map mr="fwd0" vaddr="0x2_100_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="tx_fwd_vaddr" />
        <map mr="free0" vaddr="0x2_102_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="tx_free_vaddr" />
        <map mr="pool0" vaddr="0x2_200_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="tx_pool_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_nic_driver1" vaddr="0x4_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_records_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_nic_driver1_consume" vaddr="0x4_010_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_consume_vaddr" />
        <map mr="stats_nic_driver1" vaddr="0x5_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="stats_vaddr" />
        <setvar symbol="bar_paddr" region_paddr="bar1" />
        <setvar symbol="vq_paddr" region_paddr="vq1" />
        <setvar symbol="rx_pool_paddr" region_paddr="pool1" />
        <setvar symbol="tx_pool_paddr" region_paddr="pool0" />
    </protection_domain>

    <!-- The third instance of the same binary, driving the MANAGEMENT port. It
         differs from the two above in its regions and its peer and in nothing
         else: no `nic-driver.elf` byte distinguishes a management port from a
         dataplane one, which is why this row exists rather than a fourth
         program image (pds/nic-driver's crate header).

         The two pipelines are the management port's own, so no dataplane region
         appears here and this driver can reach no frame belonging to either
         dataplane pipeline. Its receive pool it does not map at all: it takes
         `rx_pool_paddr` and hands it to the NIC, exactly as the two above do with
         theirs — the management domain reads that pool read-only, which is a
         grant to a domain that dereferences a frame and not to the one that DMAs
         into it. Its transmit pool IS mapped, for the header write, and the
         management domain now queues replies onto mgmt_tx_fwd, so this driver's
         transmit side carries traffic rather than existing for the sake of one
         binary. -->
    <protection_domain name="nic_driver2" priority="1" stack_size="0x8000">
        <program_image path="nic-driver.elf" />
        <map mr="ecam2" vaddr="0x10_000_000" perms="rw" cached="false" setvar_vaddr="ecam_vaddr" />
        <map mr="bar2" vaddr="0x10_100_000" perms="rw" cached="false" setvar_vaddr="bar_vaddr" />
        <map mr="vq2" vaddr="0x10_200_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="vq_vaddr" />
        <map mr="mgmt_rx_fwd" vaddr="0x2_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="rx_fwd_vaddr" />
        <map mr="mgmt_rx_free" vaddr="0x2_002_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="rx_free_vaddr" />
        <map mr="mgmt_tx_fwd" vaddr="0x2_100_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="tx_fwd_vaddr" />
        <map mr="mgmt_tx_free" vaddr="0x2_102_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="tx_free_vaddr" />
        <map mr="mgmt_tx_pool" vaddr="0x2_200_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="tx_pool_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_nic_driver2" vaddr="0x4_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_records_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_nic_driver2_consume" vaddr="0x4_010_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_consume_vaddr" />
        <map mr="stats_nic_driver2" vaddr="0x5_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="stats_vaddr" />
        <setvar symbol="bar_paddr" region_paddr="bar2" />
        <setvar symbol="vq_paddr" region_paddr="vq2" />
        <setvar symbol="rx_pool_paddr" region_paddr="mgmt_rx_pool" />
        <setvar symbol="tx_pool_paddr" region_paddr="mgmt_tx_pool" />
    </protection_domain>

    <!-- The console domain: the one holder of the serial controller.

         =================================================================
         `<ioport>` ENTERED THIS SYSTEM HERE (ENG-1, SCM-6)
         =================================================================

         Memory mappings and notification channels were the whole of what this
         description granted until this row. It is neither: it places an
         X86IOPort CAPABILITY in the console domain's CNode, and a capability is
         a thing the domain INVOKES. It does not unlock an instruction. seL4 leaves the TSS I/O
         permission bitmap denying every port and never edits it, so `in` and
         `out` executed inside any protection domain raise #GP however this
         file is written; the ports below are reached only by invoking this
         capability through `seL4_X86_IOPort_In8` / `seL4_X86_IOPort_Out8`,
         which is what `pds/console/src/com1.rs` does and why that file needs
         no `unsafe`.

         READ THAT BEFORE ADDING THE NEXT `<ioport>`. The console driver was
         first built on the opposite reading — that this element hands a domain
         a window of the I/O permission bitmap — so it held the capability and
         executed `out %al,(%dx)` against 0x3F9, the first write of the UART
         init sequence. It faulted with #GP, on a grant that was present and
         correct. The grant makes the invocation legal, never the instruction.

         The three attributes are exactly what the Microkit 2.3.0 tool accepts
         — `id`, `addr`, `size`, and nothing else — and the tool refuses the
         element outright off x86, which is the platform assumption CONCEPT §3
         already fixes.

         WHAT IT GRANTS: eight consecutive I/O ports, 0x3F8 through 0x3FF, the
         PC-compatible COM1 window. That is the whole of the first 16550
         controller: its data register, interrupt enable, FIFO control /
         interrupt identification, line control, modem control, line status,
         modem status and scratch. `crates/uart-16550` uses five of the eight
         and forms every address as `COM1_BASE | offset` with the base's
         alignment to an eight-port window asserted at build time, so no address
         it can form leaves this row.

         WHAT STAYS WITHHELD, and it is nearly everything an I/O-port grant
         could be: no other port in the 65536-port space, so no PCI
         configuration address/data pair at 0xCF8/0xCFC (which would be a second
         path to every device's configuration space, next to the ECAM mappings
         the drivers hold), no PS/2 controller, no PIC, no PIT, no CMOS/RTC, no
         debug port. The CMOS pair belongs to the clock domain above and to it
         alone; this domain cannot read or stop the clock, and that domain
         cannot write the line an operator reads its result on. The drivers and
         the forwarder hold zero ports between them, so an attacker who reaches
         either reaches no port instruction that will execute.

         WHY ONE DOMAIN OWNS IT. The device is unsynchronised and has one
         register file, so several writers interleave their bytes into one
         another and a console line arrives spliced with a second domain's. A
         capability held by exactly one domain is what makes that
         unrepresentable rather than unlikely — and it is a claim about THIS
         window rather than about the port space, which the clock domain's grant
         above shares nothing with. In the RELEASE kernel
         configuration this domain is then the only writer of 0x3F8 in the whole
         system and the property is exact. In the DEBUG configuration it is not:
         that kernel is built with CONFIG_PRINTING and writes the same port for
         its own boot banner and fault reports. That is accepted — the kernel
         prints on boot and on faults, not per record — but it is the reason the
         single-writer claim is stated of the shipped profile.

         WHAT IT MAPS: fourteen log regions in seven pairs, and the perms are the
         whole of the argument, as they are for the forwarder's `cfg`/`cfgack`.
         Each `log_<domain>` is READ-ONLY here: this domain renders those
         records and must read them, and a console that could also write one
         could attribute a line to a domain that never emitted it. Each
         `log_<domain>_consume` is read-write because how far the console has
         read is this domain's own statement about itself, and it is the only
         domain that may make it. See the note beside those regions.

         It holds no pool, no dataplane ring, no configuration region, no ECAM
         page and no BAR window, so a compromised console reaches no frame, no
         NIC, and no configuration — the same shape of isolation the
         configuration domain has, in the other direction.

         PRIORITY 1, EQUAL TO THE DRIVERS, and that is a decision rather than a
         leftover. This domain never leaves `init` for the same reason the
         drivers do not: Microkit has no periodic wakeup, and a `notified`-driven
         console would have to return to the event loop after each activation,
         so a boot transcript longer than the UART's 16-byte FIFO would stall
         until some unrelated domain happened to log again — and the boot
         transcript is the whole purpose of a console. It therefore busy-polls,
         which makes its priority a scheduling question rather than a
         formality. Above the drivers it would preempt the dataplane for as long
         as it held the CPU, and at 115200 baud a byte occupies the line for
         about 87 microseconds, so a burst of a few lines is milliseconds of
         stalled forwarding. Equal to them, seL4 round-robins the three at
         priority 1 and the console's share is a slice rather than a stall. The
         forwarder at 2 and the configuration domain at 3 still preempt it, so
         nothing on a notification path is delayed by it at all.

         The improvement, recorded rather than built: an interrupt-driven
         transmitter, which would remove the polling entirely. It needs the
         system's first `<irq>` — another new capability class — and is not
         worth bundling into the change that introduces the first `<ioport>`. -->
    <protection_domain name="console" priority="1" stack_size="0x4000">
        <program_image path="console.elf" />
        <ioport id="0" addr="0x3f8" size="8" />
        <map mr="log_forwarder" vaddr="0x4_000_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_forwarder_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_forwarder_consume" vaddr="0x4_010_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_forwarder_consume_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_nic_driver0" vaddr="0x4_100_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_nic_driver0_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_nic_driver0_consume" vaddr="0x4_110_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_nic_driver0_consume_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_nic_driver1" vaddr="0x4_200_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_nic_driver1_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_nic_driver1_consume" vaddr="0x4_210_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_nic_driver1_consume_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_config" vaddr="0x4_300_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_config_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_config_consume" vaddr="0x4_310_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_config_consume_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_clock" vaddr="0x4_400_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_clock_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_clock_consume" vaddr="0x4_410_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_clock_consume_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_nic_driver2" vaddr="0x4_500_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_nic_driver2_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_nic_driver2_consume" vaddr="0x4_510_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_nic_driver2_consume_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_management" vaddr="0x4_600_000" perms="r" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_management_vaddr" />
        <map mr="log_management_consume" vaddr="0x4_610_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="log_management_consume_vaddr" />
        <map mr="stats_console" vaddr="0x5_000_000" perms="rw" cached="true" setvar_vaddr="stats_vaddr" />
    </protection_domain>

    <!-- Both dataplane channels carry notifications in one direction only, so
         only that direction is granted: a driver signals the forwarder that
         frames are queued (pds/nic-driver, `PEER.notify()`), and the forwarder
         answers nothing back — it drains both pipelines on any wakeup and
         returns, and the drivers could not receive a notification anyway, since
         they never leave `init` and so never reach the Microkit event loop.
         `notify` defaults to true on an `<end>`, which would leave the
         forwarder holding a send capability on each driver's notification that
         no code path uses. -->
    <channel>
        <end pd="nic_driver0" id="0" />
        <end pd="forwarder" id="0" notify="false" />
    </channel>
    <channel>
        <end pd="nic_driver1" id="0" />
        <end pd="forwarder" id="1" notify="false" />
    </channel>

    <!-- The management port's channel, granted in one direction for exactly the
         reason the two driver channels above are: nic_driver2 signals that
         frames are queued, and the management domain answers nothing back — it
         drains the pipeline on any wakeup and returns, and the driver could not
         receive a notification anyway, never leaving `init`. `notify` defaults to
         true on an `<end>`, which would leave the management domain holding a
         send capability on a driver that has no event loop to observe it. -->
    <channel>
        <end pd="nic_driver2" id="0" />
        <end pd="management" id="0" notify="false" />
    </channel>

    <!-- The one channel in this system granted in BOTH directions, and it is a
         decision rather than a `notify` nobody thought about — which is why
         both ends state it rather than leaning on Microkit's default of true.

         Applying a configuration is a two-phase commit, and each phase is a
         signal the other end cannot infer. The config domain offers a
         generation and signals; the forwarder re-checks every field of it,
         stages a table, writes its acknowledgement and signals BACK, because
         the config domain has no other way to learn that the generation is safe
         to release — it cannot poll, having no loop to poll from, and it must
         not release a generation the consumer has refused. The config domain
         then publishes the commit and signals again, because the forwarder
         switches tables between two polls and would otherwise not run one until
         the next frame arrived — a node whose configuration lands only once
         traffic starts is a node that is misconfigured for exactly as long as
         it is idle.

         The two driver channels remain one-directional for the reason stated
         above them, and nothing here changes that: this pair grants the
         forwarder a send capability on the config domain alone. -->
    <channel>
        <end pd="config" id="0" notify="true" />
        <end pd="forwarder" id="2" notify="true" />
    </channel>

    <!-- THE CONSOLE DOMAIN HAS NO CHANNEL AT ALL, IN EITHER DIRECTION, AND ITS
         ABSENCE IS A DECISION.

         A DOMAIN THAT NEVER BLOCKS NEEDS NO WAKEUP. The console busy-polls the
         five rings and never leaves `init`, for the reason set out above it, so
         it never reaches the Microkit event loop and could not observe a
         notification if one were sent. It discovers work the only way a polling
         domain can: by reading the cursor each writing domain publishes, which
         it does on every pass regardless. A send capability on this domain
         would therefore be authority granted for nothing — a widening of five
         writers' reach with no path that consumes it (ENG-1) — so no channel is
         declared, and the machinery that fed one is gone rather than left
         dormant (ENG-7).

         The direction that stays refused on its own terms: nothing here grants
         the console a send capability on any domain. A console that could
         notify a driver would hold a wakeup capability on the domain it is
         supposed to be reporting about, and a domain woken by whatever is
         printing about it is a control path dressed as a diagnostic one. That
         remains true whatever replaces the polling.

         When the console becomes interrupt-driven — the improvement recorded
         above it — the wakeup it needs is an `<irq>` from the controller, not a
         notification from a writer: what a transmitter-empty interrupt says is
         "the line can take more bytes", which is the thing the polling loop is
         actually waiting for. A writer's notification would say only "a record
         exists", which the cursor already says. -->
</system>
